  "biography": {
    "plain_text": "John Harvey Reilly Jr. was an American businessman who led United Refrigeration, the Philadelphia company established by his parents, for thirty-five years. Born in Teaneck, New Jersey, he attended Malvern Preparatory School and Villanova University. He remained chairman and chief executive of the refrigeration and air-conditioning group until his death in 2012.",
    "html": "<p>John Harvey Reilly Jr. was an American businessman who led United Refrigeration, the Philadelphia company established by his parents, for thirty-five years. Born in Teaneck, New Jersey, he attended Malvern Preparatory School and Villanova University. He remained chairman and chief executive of the refrigeration and air-conditioning group until his death in 2012.</p>\r\n",
    "word_count": 52,
    "confidence": {
      "score": 92,
      "band": "high",
      "reason": "Family obituary and contemporary buyer announcement support bounded historical narrative."
    },
    "source_ids": [
      "S1",
      "S4"
    ]
  },
  "long_biography": {
    "plain_text": "During refrigeration executive John Reilly Jr.’s final years in charge, United Refrigeration refocused its international operations. The company agreed to sell its French and British wholesale businesses to G&L Beijer so it could concentrate on North America. The transaction covered two businesses supplying refrigeration and cooling equipment, extending the buyer’s distribution network in both countries.\n\nReilly’s interests outside work included flying his own aircraft, scuba diving and travelling with little luggage. He asked companions to take only what they could carry. His enthusiasm for machinery continued on the water, where he captained his own boats and worked on their engines.",
    "html": "<p>During refrigeration executive John Reilly Jr.’s final years in charge, United Refrigeration refocused its international operations. The company agreed to sell its French and British wholesale businesses to G&amp;L Beijer so it could concentrate on North America. The transaction covered two businesses supplying refrigeration and cooling equipment, extending the buyer’s distribution network in both countries.</p>\r\n<p>Reilly’s interests outside work included flying his own aircraft, scuba diving and travelling with little luggage. He asked companions to take only what they could carry. His enthusiasm for machinery continued on the water, where he captained his own boats and worked on their engines.</p>\r\n",
